    "product_id": "f2c38b5a-e26b-43c1-b95b-1a2ea86cce26",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 061031\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n331 AM MST Sun Sep 6 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Aside from some light shower chances across Joshua Tree National\n  Park today mostly dry and quiet weather conditions are expected\n  through the first half of this week. \n\n- Thunderstorm chances will start to increase, mainly across\n  south-central and eastern Arizona, by the latter half of the \n  upcoming workweek. \n\n- Temperatures will be near to around 5 degrees above normal \n  through the upcoming week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nEarly this morning reveals that the overall synoptic pattern remains \nlargely unchanged with the omega block in tact. The ridge of high \npressure remains in place across the central US with troughing along \nboth coasts, with the low pressure centers over the Pacific NW and \nNew England. Hurricane Marie also continues to spin well off the \ncoast of the Baja Peninsula. This pattern continues to support \nmid and upper level moisture advection into the region, with the \nhighest moisture content across SE CA and SW AZ. This can be seen \non the SPC Mesoscale Analysis, where PWATs are around 1.5-1.7\" \nacross SE CA and SW AZ. This forcing, in combination with the \nincreased moisture, is resulting in some light shower activity, \nfor our CWA, mainly across eastern Riverside County. The SPC \nMesoscale Analysis also shows little to no CAPE, so no \nthunderstorm activity is expected. Throughout the day today, the \nhigh will begin to retrograde westward with the trough across \nwestern CONUS beginning to lift northeastward. Additionally, \nHurricane Marie will continue to move northwestward. The \ncombination of these three things will result in the better \nmoisture plume moving northwestward. So, the best shower chances, \nfor our CWA, today will be confined to Joshua Tree National Park. \nThere will be little to no instability so no thunderstorms are \nexpected. Little to no rainfall accumulations are also expected. \nThe best rainfall today will be north of our CWA into San \nBernardino County in CA, into the Las Vegas area, and NW AZ. With \nthe shower activity across this region, outflow winds from the \nnorthwest are expected to impact much of the lower deserts during\nthe afternoon and early evening, with peak gusts of 20-30 mph \nlikely.\n\nHeading into the beginning of the workweek, the subtropical high \nwill continue to retrograde westward. H5 heights over our area will \ngo from 588-591 dm today to 591-594 dm for the beginning of the \nworkweek. This will result in a return of southwesterly flow which \nwill bring in drier air. PWATs will decrease to 1.3-1.6\" area \nwide. In addition to the decrease in moisture, with high pressure \nmoving back in dry and quiet conditions are on tap to start the \nworkweek. Temperature wise, with high pressure building back in \ntemperatures will increase slightly through Tuesday. For areas \nacross SE CA they should see temperatures a couple degrees below \nnormal today due to the increased cloud cover from the increased \nmid and high level moisture. Otherwise, lower desert afternoon \nhigh temperatures are forecasted to be in the 100-108 degree range\nand for the higher terrain, in the 92-102 degree range. \nTemperatures of this magnitude are around 2-4 degrees above \nnormal, which results in Minor to Moderate HeatRisk area wide.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nHeading into the later half of the workweek, ensemble model guidance \nhas come into slightly better agreement on the overall synoptic \npattern. The models have come into better agreement on the placement \nof the features, but still differ on the overall strength of the \nfeatures. Both the GEFS and the EPS show a shortwave trough across \nnorther CONUS and southern Canada, a broad subtropical ridge \nstretching across much of the lower 2/3 of CONUS, along with a \nclosed low off the coast of northern California. The EPS continues \nto be the stronger solution, with a stronger closed low in the \nPacific and a stronger subtropical high. These changes can be seen \nin the now slightly smaller temperature spread in the NBM \ninterquartile range, however, the spread remains large enough (5-7 \ndegrees) that you can tell the models are still in some \ndisagreement. The GEFS show the high at peak strength (H5 heights of \n594-597 dm) during the middle of the week before weakening (H5 \nheights of 591-594 dm) and moving overhead on Thursday. The high \nlooks to continue to weaken (h5 heights of 588-592 dm) heading into \nthe weekend. So temperatures are forecasted to remain slightly above \nnormal during the middle of the week before beginning a slight \ncooling trend to slightly below normal for the end of the workweek \nand into the weekend.  \n\nAs for shower and storm chances, the ensemble members continue to \nshow those chances starting to increase, for at least portions of \nthe CWA, beginning on Wednesday. PWATs look to increase to around \n1.5-1.9\" for the latter half of the week, so moisture should not be \na limiting factor. With the high forecasted to move overhead on \nThursday, rain chances will likely be limited to the higher terrain \nareas across eastern Arizona on Wednesday and Thursday. Rain chances \nmay extend into the lower deserts of south-central Arizona heading \ninto the weekend. However, the current WPC PoPs of 30-50%, for \nFriday into Saturday, seem rather aggressive, especially this far \nout. WPC's Saturday PoPs of 10-30% seem more reasonable.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1000Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nMinor uncertainty in wind direction will be the only weather\nconcern through Monday morning under periods of thick mid/high\ncigs and areas of virga. Instead of winds fully shifting east this\nmorning at KPHX, they may instead just become variable for several\nhours. A return to a pronounced west direction should occur by\nlate morning with some modest gusts (20-25 kts at most) late this\nafternoon into early evening.  \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nThick mid/high cigs with virga/sprinkles will persist through this\nevening, albeit with little to no operational impact. Confidence\nis good that light W/SW winds will gradually veer to the NW by\nlate morning with gusts 20-25kt developing mid/late afternoon. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nTemperatures will be slightly below normal across SE CA today.\nOtherwise, temperatures will be near to slightly above normal\nthrough the workweek. Rain chances today will be confined to\nJoshua Tree National Park, with chances for wetting rainfall very\nlow. MinRH's will be around 20-30% area wide through much of this\nupcoming week with good overnight recovery. Some elevated \nafternoon/early evening breeziness out of the northwest is \nexpected today with peak gusts of 20-30 mph. A more typical \ndiurnal wind pattern with some occasional afternoon upslope \nbreeziness can be expected through much of this coming week.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Berislavich\nLONG TERM...Berislavich \nAVIATION...Whittock/18 \nFIRE WEATHER...Lojero/Berislavich\n",
